      "id": "qGK66tvIIDkbcH6Cq",
      "name": "Knot-Tying",
      "reference": "Sol:15",
      "reference_highlight": "Knot-Tying",
      "local_notes": "**Defaults:** Relevant penetration technique-2 or Erotic Art-4  \n**Prerequisites:** Erotic Art 12+ and Exotic Genitalia; the Exotic Genitalia must actually include a knot, bulb, locking ridge, barbs, or another structure capable of forming a lock  \n**Maximum:** Relevant penetration technique  \n**Stimulation:** **+2 to the base technique**\n\nThis is the **giving-side** counterpart to Mating-Lock Management. The active partner deliberately aligns and seats their locking anatomy so that the lock engages correctly.\n\n**Success:** The lock engages cleanly and adds +2 Stimulation to the recipient.  \n**Failure:** The underlying penetration technique continues normally, but the lock does not properly engage.  \n**Critical failure:** The recipient must immediately roll **Mating-Lock Management or Stuffing**. Failure on that roll invokes the appropriate Stuffing-type painful/injury consequences.\n\n**Critical success:** The Knot-Tying contribution becomes **+4 instead of +2**. Do not double the underlying penetration technique unless that technique also critically succeeds.\n\nKnot-Tying is an add-on to a relevant penetration technique and does not require Double Play merely to combine with that base position.\n\nIf a creature's lock activates automatically at orgasm, Knot-Tying represents getting the anatomy correctly aligned **before** the automatic lock occurs.\n\nGCS use: choose one default variant for a purchased technique. A maximum tied to another skill, or the lower of two skills, must be checked against the named skill(s); the native limit field is relative to the selected default. Apply situational modifiers to the roll, not as bonuses to all Erotic Art techniques.\n\nGCS selection required: replace @Relevant Penetration Technique@ with the exact name of the applicable learned technique. For a lower-of rule, select the lower current level and recheck after either technique changes.",
