    {
      "id": "qEOyKjXiwQJ1ks2TP",
      "name": "Multiple Partners",
      "reference": "SexAndPreg:88",
      "reference_highlight": "Multiple Partners",
      "local_notes": "MANUAL SPECIALIZATION: choose two specific techniques. Default is the lowest technique used -4; maximum the lowest technique used.\n\nSeptember reference: Defaults Lowest technique used-4; prerequisites Must specialize in two selected techniques; maximum Lowest technique; Stimulation Derived from constituent techniques. Coordinates two or more partners simultaneously. Book's “Spit Roast” example uses ST-based Multiple Partners rolls while the central participant uses Double Play.\n\nAlternative default entry: choose one default for this technique, not several separate purchases. Where the named maximum depends on a different skill or the lower of two skills, enforce that cross-skill maximum manually; GCS limit is relative to the selected default.\n\nGCS selection required: replace @Lowest Selected Technique@ with the exact name of the applicable learned technique. For a lower-of rule, select the lower current level and recheck after either technique changes.\n\nGCS specialization: enter the two selected techniques. Use the lower of their current levels for @Lowest Selected Technique@; the default is −4 and the maximum is that lowest technique. Recheck after either underlying technique changes.\n\n**Spit Roast — composite configuration from the notes**\n\n**Participants:** Three  \n**Techniques Required:** Multiple Partners for both outside participants; Double Play for the central participant; appropriate constituent sexual techniques.\n\nA Spit Roast is not a separate technique. It is a coordinated use of Multiple Partners and Double Play. The two outside participants each make a **ST-based Multiple Partners** roll, representing their ability to support, position, and coordinate the central participant. The central participant makes **Double Play**, specialized for one mouth-based maneuver and one vaginal or anal-position maneuver.\n\nWhen Double Play is used in this configuration, its two component techniques may affect different partners. Resolve Stimulation separately for each component rather than adding both values to a single recipient.\n\nFor anal penetration, make any required **Anal accommodation** roll separately. Anal itself supplies no Stimulation; use an appropriate penetrative position as the second component of Double Play.\n\nFailure by one outside participant means that participant loses proper coordination/support; the GM may end the configuration or impose -2 on the other participants' associated techniques until position is restored. Critical failure may cause the central participant to be dropped or badly mispositioned, at the GM's discretion.\n\nFailure of the central participant's Double Play means the two actions aren't successfully coordinated and should use the normal failure results of the constituent techniques.",
      "tags": [
        "Sexual"
      ],
      "difficulty": "h",
      "default": {
        "type": "skill",
        "name": {
          "compare": "is",
          "qualifier": "@Lowest Selected Technique@"
        },
        "modifier": -4
      },
      "limit": 0,
      "points": 2,
      "specialization": "@First Technique@ + @Second Technique@"
    },
